1. Child Process - fork()
  fork() creates a copy of the current process called a child process. 
  After fork(), both parent and child run the same code but have different PIDs. 
  The return value tells them apart — fork() returns 0 to the child and the child's PID to the parent. They run independently after the split.

2. Handling Common Signals
  Signals are notifications sent to a process when something happens (Ctrl+C, kill command, etc.). 
  Common ones are SIGINT (interrupt), SIGTERM (terminate), SIGSEGV (segfault), SIGKILL (force kill).

3. Kernel Crashes
  A kernel crash (panic) happens when the OS hits an unrecoverable error and stops to prevent further damage. 
  Common causes are null pointer dereference, stack overflow, hardware failure, or a buggy driver. 
  Unlike user-space crashes, a kernel crash brings down the entire system.

4. Time Complexity
  Time complexity measures how the runtime of a program grows as input size increases. 
  It is expressed using Big-O notation like O(1), O(n), O(n²).

5. Locking Mechanism - Mutex / Spinlock
  When multiple threads access shared data at the same time, it causes a race condition. 
  A mutex makes a thread wait (sleep) until the lock is free — good for long waits. Mutex is preferred in most user-space programs.
  A spinlock keeps the thread busy-waiting in a loop checking the lock — good for very short waits. Spinlock is used in kernel code where sleeping is not allowed.
